AI image tools do not all count the same thing. Midjourney sells time on its graphics processors, Adobe Firefly sells generative credits, and Canva gives a monthly AI allowance. That makes a plain price comparison misleading: the cheapest headline price is not always the cheapest image. This guide sets out how Midjourney, Adobe Firefly and Canva meter AI image generation, using what each publishes on its own pricing and help pages. It does not judge image quality, because we have not run the three tools side by side, and it does not compare the tools for any one style of image. A disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the vendor pages it could open, not by using the products ourselves. Claims the reviewer could not confirm were removed. Prices, credit costs and allowances change often, so check the live pages before you pay. Adobe's pages that we could read list credits but not dollar prices, so we do not quote Adobe prices here.

## How each one meters images

| | Midjourney | Adobe Firefly | Canva |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| What you buy | Fast GPU hours, plus unlimited slower Relax mode on Standard and up | Monthly generative credits; standard features unlimited on paid plans | A monthly AI allowance shared across AI tools |
| Free option | No free plan listed on its pricing page | Limited free daily generations | Canva Free includes a small AI allowance |
| Paid entry | Basic: $10 a month, or $8 billed annually | Firefly Standard: 2,000 credits a month | Canva Pro, with a larger AI allowance than Free |
| Heavy use | Mega: $120 a month, or $96 billed annually, 60 Fast hours | Firefly Premium: 50,000 credits a month | AI Pass add-on for paid plans, at extra cost |

![Column chart of Midjourney monthly plan prices with annual billing, from 8 dollars for Basic with 3.3 Fast hours to 96 dollars for Mega with 60 Fast hours](/images/site1/ai-image-generators-pricing-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from Midjourney's published plan page.*

## Midjourney

Midjourney's plan page lists four subscription tiers. Monthly prices are $10 for Basic, $30 for Standard, $60 for Pro and $120 for Mega, and paying for a year upfront takes 20 percent off, which works out to $8, $24, $48 and $96 a month. Fast GPU time is 3.3 hours a month on Basic, then 15, 30 and 60 hours.

Dividing price by Fast time, our own arithmetic, gives about $2 per Fast hour on Standard, Pro and Mega at monthly billing, $1.60 at annual billing, and roughly $3 for Basic at monthly billing ($2.40 annually). Midjourney sells extra Fast time at $4 an hour, so every tier costs less per hour than a top-up. Midjourney's plans page says purchased hours expire after 60 days, and its help pages say unused monthly Fast time does not carry over.

Midjourney's help site says a prompt usually uses about one minute of GPU time, though upscales and other actions can cost more and variations less, and its pricing page says one image job produces four images. On that basis Basic's 200 minutes is about 200 jobs a month, which Midjourney's page also states as roughly 800 images. Standard, Pro and Mega add unlimited image generation in Relax mode, which does not use Fast time but takes longer. Pro and Mega add Stealth mode, which keeps your images private.

On commercial use, Midjourney's plan page says companies making more than $1,000,000 a year in gross revenue must buy the Pro or Mega plan. Read the Terms of Service for the full commercial rules.

## Adobe Firefly

Adobe's generative credits FAQ describes one credit type with two kinds of feature. Standard features, such as Generative Fill in Photoshop, are unlimited on paid Firefly plans and on Creative Cloud Pro, with the FAQ describing standard image and vector generations as unlimited. Premium features, including video, audio and partner models, spend credits.

Monthly credits for individuals, according to Adobe's FAQ, are 2,000 on Firefly Standard, 4,000 on Firefly Pro, 10,000 on Firefly Pro Plus and 50,000 on Firefly Premium. Creative Cloud Pro includes 4,000 premium credits and unlimited standard generations, while Creative Cloud Standard includes 25 credits a month. Free users get a limited number of free daily generations on a curated set of models.

For images specifically, the FAQ lists Firefly Image 5 at 10 credits per generation and Firefly Image 4 Ultra at 20 credits, so a 2,000-credit plan covers roughly 200 Image 5 generations or 100 Ultra generations, our own arithmetic. Adobe's help center also describes a special offer, with model lists current as of October 13, 2026, that makes selected models unlimited for 12 months from your plan start date. It applies to individual Firefly Pro, Pro Plus and Premium and the 4,000 to 50,000 credit plans, not to Teams or Enterprise, and it does not list Firefly Standard. Image models on the list include GPT Image 1.5 and GPT Image 2. If you are on an eligible plan, credit costs for those models may not matter during the offer, but the model list is subject to change, so check the page.

Adobe describes its Firefly video model as commercially safe in a February 2025 announcement, a claim that is Adobe's own. If commercial safety matters for your work, read Adobe's current terms for the model you plan to use.

## Canva

Canva's help center says some AI tools are used freely on paid plans and others draw from a shared monthly allowance. Creating an image with Canva AI is listed as a premium AI tool. On Canva Free and Pro Lite the allowance is small, while Pro and Nonprofits get a larger one. Pro Lite, a plan between Free and Pro, has the same AI allowance as Free and, per Canva's help page, is offered only in some countries.

Canva's help page gives the clearest figures: up to 20 uses of standard or premium AI tools on Free, and up to 200 premium uses, or up to 20 ultra uses, on Pro, which is about ten times as many premium uses (our own arithmetic). Canva's allowances and prices vary by plan and country, so confirm the current numbers on Canva's pricing page before you buy; we do not quote its dollar prices here.

Canva says its free trials of Pro and Business include only the same AI allowance as Free, and the full allowance starts when you pay. Canva's help center lists an AI Pass add-on for Pro and Teams, for an extra cost that we do not quote.

## How to choose

- **Occasional images inside designs:** Canva's allowance is bundled with a design tool you may already use, and the Free allowance lets you try it first.
- **Lots of images and exploring styles:** Midjourney's Relax mode is unlimited from Standard up, if you can wait for slower generations.
- **Editing photos and generating inside Adobe apps:** Firefly's unlimited standard features and Creative Cloud integration are the draw, with credits spent only on premium models.
- **Checking value:** divide plan price by what you will really use. Midjourney Basic at $8 and Canva's Free tier cost very different amounts per image.

Whichever you try, start with one month, since pricing and limits change often.
